⚠️ **IMPORTANT: the async settle can lose already-consumed steering texts when a competing run starts during the persist-retry window**

`_settleLeftoverSteering` is now fire-and-forget (`unawaited` at `agent_cli_input.dart:217`), and the settle-run branch awaits one persist re-attempt per entry **before** starting the run (`agent_cli_steering.dart:401` → `:404`):

```dart
for (final entry in entries) {
  final recordId = await _retrySteeringPersist(entry);
  unawaited(_steeringDelivered(entry.message, recordId, entry.panel));
}
_startRun(texts.join('\n'));
```

The pre-change settle was synchronous: the "running N message(s) now" decision and `_startRun` were one atomic block, so nothing could interleave. Now the window between the announce line and `_startRun` costs at least a microtask hop per entry — and a **full session append** (up to the #2248 session-IO bound under a slow fs) for every entry carrying a `persistError`. During that window `isBusy` is still false, so a composer submit, mail wake, or scheduled message can start a run. The settle's `_startRun(texts.join('\n'))` then hits the busy guard (`isBusy => _runStarting || isStreaming`) and refuses — **after** the FIFO entries were already consumed (`_pendingSteering.removeWhere`), the panels flipped to `delivered`, and `steering arrived after the last checkpoint — running N message(s) now` was printed. The steered texts end up in no run and no queue: silently lost, behind a "running now" line that lied — exactly the defect class this card closes.

Suggested fix: start the run first (synchronously), then fire the per-entry retry deliveries — the crash-recovery scan (`_recoverSessionSteering`) is order-independent, so JSONL record ordering does not constrain the swap. If the retry-before-run ordering is deliberate (steering record preceding the run's own records in the JSONL), the busy-guard refusal path still needs a guard: re-check `isBusy` after the awaits and re-drain/re-drop loudly instead of consuming-and-losing.

